\section{Regions and the noise floor across heads}\label{sec:S6}

This section gives the regional results for every head and the dependence of the comparison on the noise of the optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs). Throughout, D is the detectability of the dense OPM array relative to Neuromag, the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID) system, in dB (Section~\ref{sec:S1}). D is the area-weighted median over a region's cortical targets in both hemispheres, with sensor plus brain noise. The helmet constructions are described in Section~\ref{sec:S5}.

\subsection{Regions by head}\label{sec:S6.1}

In the fixed adult helmet at top contact, the dense array was ahead in every region of every head (D << F.regh_top_vs_combined_cells_range >> dB over the cells of Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}A). A smaller head exceeded the adult's value for the same region in << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_above_adult_count >> of << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_n >> cells. Of the six lobes, the temporal lobe gained most in the infant templates (<< F.regh_infant2yr_top_vs_combined_temporal >>, << F.regh_infant18mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> and << F.regh_infant12mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, against << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB in the adult).

The parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows were the adult's lowest temporal rows (<< F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_parahippocampal >> and << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB), and both were higher in every template and scaled adult. The mesial temporal group reached << F.regh_templates_scaled_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB in the templates and scaled adults but << F.regh_children_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB in the children, with child C (<< F.regh_child_c_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB) below the adult. The cingulate was the lowest row in every head (<< F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_cingulate >> dB in the adult, << F.regh_smaller_top_vs_combined_cingulate_range >> dB in the smaller heads).

The peak-channel signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) gave a different picture (sensor plus brain noise). Against the magnetometers alone, the dense array's peak-channel SNR was ahead in every head (D << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d >> dB in the adult at top contact, << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads). Against Neuromag's best channel, Neuromag led in the adult (<< F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB) and in << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d_negative_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d_range >> dB). The children were among them (<< F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >>, << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> and << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB in children A, B and C).

In the helmet scaled with the head, the regional gain was smaller, and how much smaller depended on the reference (lobes only; Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}B). Against the adult in its own scaled helmet, << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_count >> of the << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_n >> lobe cells of the smaller heads were at or below the adult's. Against the adult at top contact, << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> were (<< F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_templates_scaled_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_templates_scaled_lobe_cells_n >> for the templates and scaled adults, << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_n >> for the children). These counts compare point estimates without a tolerance. The children's lobes ranged from << F.regh_children_cfx_vs_combined_lobes_range >> dB in the scaled helmet.

Table~\ref{tab:S8} gives the regional D in the helmet fitted at the adult's gap. The adult's ordering, with frontal, precentral and lateral temporal cortex highest and cingulate and insula lowest, held in the scaled adults. The cingulate was the lowest row in every head except the children, where the insula lay just below it. The templates and children, however, reordered the upper rows. The parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows were high in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month templates and in child B, and the occipital lobe was highest in child C.

The children differed from the adult by region in both directions. Child B was below the adult in the frontal lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_frontal >> dB) and the precentral gyrus (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_precentral >> dB) but above it in the parahippocampal (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_parahippocampal >> dB) and occipital (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB) cortex. Child C was below the adult in the entorhinal cortex (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_entorhinal >> dB) but above it in the occipital lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB).

The cortical maps (Fig.~\ref{fig:S10}; main text, Fig. 6C) show where D is largest: on the lateral convexity, most on gyral crowns, with medial surfaces near zero. Region by region, then, the ordering changed with the helmet; the relative weight of helmet and region was not quantified. Heat maps and cortical maps show point estimates, and the pediatric intervals represent within-anatomy resampling only.

