\subsection{The noise floor across heads}\label{sec:S6.2}

D fell with the OPM's sensor noise in every head (Fig.~\ref{fig:S11}). At << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz, about the empty-room floor read from the recording of \citet{jas2026}, the dense array's D was << F.g3b_adult_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB in the adult and << F.g3b_smaller_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads. The site-matched array's D was negative in every head except the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_years >>-year size control (<< F.g3b_adult_asd30_matched_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB in the adult, << F.g3b_smaller_asd30_matched_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads).

The difference from the adult itself depended on the noise. At << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >> fT/√Hz the individual children were slightly below the adult (<< F.g3b_children_asd7_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB, difference of medians), and at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz they were above it (<< F.g3b_children_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB). A plausible reading, not tested here, is that the children's OPM channels, dominated by brain noise, lose less to a noisier sensor than the adult's. A noisier OPM thus leaves the adult without an established advantage first, while the smaller heads in the fixed helmet keep part of theirs.

